Factorial invocation state checks
=================================

  ./maxima-local --no-init --lisp=sbcl \
    --batch-string='run_testsuite(tests=[rtest10,rtest_gamma]);'

Run every configured Lisp and the complete core+share suite. New examples
are appended to rtest10, preserving previous problem numbers. Require the
complete test count and inspect the error summary, including Lisp errors.

FACTPLUSCOMB owns its processed-factorial list and four local helpers for
each invocation. FACTCOMB also binds GENVAR to NIL, so its rational conversions
allocate private generated symbols; copying a list of shared symbols would
still permit ORDERPOINTER to overwrite their values in another computation.
The factorial formulas and public expression representation remain intact.

The helper intercepts FACTEXPAND before an outer root scans its factorials.
An inner call processes x! either serially, in a forced worker, or while
throwing to a handler inside the hook. The outer (x+1)*x! must still simplify
to (x+1)!. Instrumentation restores FACTEXPAND after joining the worker.
A separate check verifies that caller-owned GENVAR symbols retain their
values. Run these checks in an otherwise idle Maxima process.

Ninety-six generated cases compare symbolic products/powers with their
expected shifted factorial, verify input preservation, then substitute a
positive integer and compare with independent exact integer multiplication.
Native/fallback batches exercise 24 distinct jobs in eight rounds. Examples
also cover quotients, constants, symbolic expressions and factorial powers.
These invocation boundaries do not certify every rational/simplifier entry
point or arbitrary concurrently shared CRE inputs.
